WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.950
Good morning Hank it’s
Tuesday May 18th

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.790
which means the Anthropocene
Reviewed book is out today.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.970
I have to do a TV thing
I am somewhat nervous about.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.349
But first, a while back I
discussed the strange joy of

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:14.500
having my books go to places
I will never go.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.500
Like starting

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.190
today the book has a life
separate from its author and it

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.330
gets to go on all kinds of adventures
around the world forging

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.199
whatever connections it can from
whoever reads it.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:24.199
Anyways,

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:25.800
in response to my talking about
that, Nerdfighters Jim and Rhett

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.759
made theanthropocenereviewed.com,
a domain name I am now very

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:32.720
grateful I never bought, and
built a website where people

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.020
can share the approximate
location of their books and also

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.060
review that location.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.050
It has
been such a joy to watch the

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.910
books start to travel and this
morning to start to read your

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.090
reviews of your locations.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.350
So if you have a moment go

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.030
to the anthropocenereviewed.com
and let people know where your

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.030
book ended up.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.060
Also share a
picture of your signature if you

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.190
got an autographed one.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.560
I’m very interested to know where

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:54.560
all the signatures went.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.080
Ok I have to go do this TV thing.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.850
Cut to me looking nervous.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:00:59.850
Here we are, I’m getting my

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.120
makeup done Hank.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.840
This is my favorite thing. 
It feels like a nice

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.480
person is putting a protective
shield on me.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.860
That’s how I always feel when I’m
getting my makeup done.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.860
And I’m ready.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:10.860
There’s also a
discord channel where you can

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.660
discuss the book with other
people in the community,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.660
link in the dooblydoo.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.050
Also,
reviews are coming in and

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.930
so far they are very nice,
like San Francisco chronicle

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.680
said, I want to make sure I
quote this correctly:

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.990
“The Anthropocene Reviewed is the
perfect book to read over lunch

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.490
or to keep on your nightstand
whenever you need a reminder

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.740
of what it is to feel small and
human in the best possible way.”

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.780
By the way that review also says,
Green is a ravenous and tender

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.870
polymath.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.140
Which is very kind even
though I feel like proper polymaths

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.730
know how to say that word, and
I’ve never been totally confident

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.280
about my pronunciation of it.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.280
Ok now I really have to

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.280
do this TV thing.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.550
Here’s a
picture of Drew Barrymore

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.750
hugging my book after he said
she loved it so that was nice.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:48.750
I’m back.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.440
I hope that TV thing
went ok, but the most important

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.050
thing is that it’s over and I am
where I belong, in my basement.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.830
Also another great review
just came in, I hope you don’t

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.170
mind if I read a little bit of it, it
made me really happy.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.170
“Lyrical

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:02.840
and beautiful, funny and hopefully,
intricate and entertaining all

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.890
at once, Green may have made
his name by writing fiction and

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.250
for good reason, but this first
foray into non fiction is his

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.810
most mature, compelling, and
beautifully written book yet.”

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:13.810
What else?

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.010
The virtual tour
started last night with the

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.130
brilliant Clint Smith, and it was
so fun that for a little while I

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.120
forgot I was even on zoom.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.150
The tour rolls on tonight with

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.800
special guest radio lab co-host
Latif Nasser and Sarah Urist Green.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.440
And then on Wednesday with
Hank and Sarah, and then on

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.880
Saturday with Ashley C Ford
and Sarah.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:30.880
It’s going to be

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:31.880
a lot of fun!

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:32.880
Tickets
in the dooblydoo.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.790
It’s been a long 3 years and a
really, really long year and a

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.510
half and I’m just so grateful that the
book is here.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.510
I wrote the book

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.970
because I wanted to write my
way back to wonder, and hope,

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.910
and sustained attention.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.400
And it
gave me that gift.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.440
And I hope it can give you
something of that gift too.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.710
Last thing from me, and it may not
be easy for me to say, but Hank this

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.650
book started with you.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.280
It started
with big, sprawling conversations

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.720
about our human moment and
how to make sense of it, and it

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.140
started with belly laughs on a
tour bus during a cross country

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.209
road trip.

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:10.530
Like so much of my
work and life, it starts with you.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.849
There’s a long standing inside
joke around here that the

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:16.590
protagonists of all my novels
are only children because I

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.220
don’t like to write about siblings,
but that’s not true.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.530
The truth is that

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.099
writing about siblings is too
close to my vulnerable and

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:28.310
beating heart.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.250
This book finally
does have a main character with a sibling,

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.390
and you are at the beginning and
middle and end of it, telling me

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.540
what I need to hear with wit and
empathy and love, just like in real life.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.460
I loved writing about you almost as
much as I love and miss being

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:48.900
able to hang out with you so thank you,
thank you for inspiring this book

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.020
and for supporting me every step of
the way.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:53.840
Thank you, thank you,

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.000
thank you Hank I’ll
see you on Friday.

